def register(add):
    def A(title,author,year,era,genre,setting,hook,cast,frames,q,answer,src,note='',date=None):
        url=f'https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/{src}' if isinstance(src,int) else src
        add(title,author,year,date or str(year),era,genre,setting,hook,cast,frames,q,answer,source=url,note=note)
    A("Middlemarch","George Eliot",1871,"victorian","Realist novel","A provincial English town before the Reform Act","Large hopes must learn to work within other people's lives.","Dorothea Brooke=Idealist seeking meaningful work|Casaubon=Scholar she marries|Will Ladislaw=Younger man who understands Dorothea|Tertius Lydgate=Doctor seeking reform|Rosamond Vincy=His socially ambitious wife|Bulstrode=Banker with a concealed past", '''
The ideal marriage imagined~Dorothea mistakes Casaubon's learned project for a means of doing meaningful work. Her desire for purpose makes her overlook the emotional limits of the husband she chooses.~Dorothea Brooke,Casaubon~book~IDEALS can misread people
A new doctor arrives~Lydgate wants to improve medicine in Middlemarch. His ambitions encounter local habits, financial dependence and the difficulty of persuading a community to value unfamiliar forms of knowledge.~Tertius Lydgate~village~REFORM needs more than expertise
The marriage disappoints~Casaubon's insecurity and unfinished scholarship constrain Dorothea. Will's attention offers another kind of understanding, but suspicion and family expectation complicate the connection.~Dorothea Brooke,Casaubon,Will Ladislaw~chains~DEVOTION meets limitation
Another household creates pressure~Lydgate marries Rosamond, whose expectations of status and comfort differ from his professional hopes. Debt converts their private disagreement into a threat to his medical independence.~Tertius Lydgate,Rosamond Vincy~coins~DIFFERENT dreams share one budget
The past enters the town~Bulstrode's concealed wrongdoing is exposed and entangles Lydgate through financial assistance. Reputation travels across relationships, making even partial association capable of damaging trust.~Bulstrode,Tertius Lydgate~letter~DEPENDENCE spreads consequence
Dorothea sees another person's pain~Despite her own disappointment and a misleading encounter, Dorothea reaches toward Rosamond with sympathy. Understanding another life becomes a more practical moral achievement than the grandeur she once imagined.~Dorothea Brooke,Rosamond Vincy~heart~SYMPATHY interrupts resentment
Lives continue beyond their hopes~Dorothea chooses Will despite an inheritance penalty; Lydgate's reforms narrow under circumstance. The finale values ordinary, partly hidden acts whose effects extend beyond public fame.~Dorothea Brooke,Will Ladislaw,Tertius Lydgate~garden~UNRECORDED care also changes the world''',"What connects Dorothea's and Lydgate's stories?","Idealistic ambitions are constrained by mistaken marriages, money and the needs of other people.",145,"Published in parts 1871–1872. Multiple plots interlock; these frames follow two central strands.",date="1871–1872")
    A("The Mill on the Floss","George Eliot",1860,"victorian","Tragic realist novel","Dorlcote Mill and St. Ogg’s","A sister's need for love collides with the terms of belonging.","Maggie Tulliver=Intelligent and emotionally intense|Tom Tulliver=Brother committed to duty and respectability|Philip Wakem=Sensitive friend|Stephen Guest=Attracted to Maggie|Lucy Deane=Maggie's cousin", '''
The siblings inhabit different worlds~Maggie's intelligence and impulsiveness contrast with Tom's narrower confidence. Their attachment is strong, but affection becomes difficult when Tom expects obedience rather than understanding.~Maggie Tulliver,Tom Tulliver~home~LOVE can impose conditions
The family loses its footing~Financial disaster takes the mill from the Tullivers. Mr. Tulliver's resentment of Wakem becomes an inherited conflict, restricting Maggie's connection with Wakem's son Philip.~Maggie Tulliver,Tom Tulliver,Philip Wakem~coins~A DEBT becomes a family boundary
Renunciation promises peace~Maggie turns toward self-denial in an effort to contain her needs. Reading and inward discipline offer a way to bear disappointment, but they cannot remove her desire for affection and intellectual companionship.~Maggie Tulliver~book~SUPPRESSION is not an easy cure
Philip understands her~Their secret friendship gives Maggie attention and sympathy denied elsewhere. Tom forbids it, making loyalty to family conflict with a relationship that recognises her individual life.~Maggie Tulliver,Philip Wakem,Tom Tulliver~heart~UNDERSTANDING crosses a prohibition
Stephen brings another temptation~Maggie and Stephen feel a powerful attraction despite his connection to Lucy. A boat journey carries them beyond a safe return, converting emotional hesitation into a public crisis.~Maggie Tulliver,Stephen Guest,Lucy Deane~river~DESIRE puts other lives at risk
The return is judged~Maggie refuses to marry Stephen and returns, but the community reads her conduct through scandal. Tom rejects her, while limited acts of kindness reveal a more humane response than public condemnation.~Maggie Tulliver,Tom Tulliver~gate~CHOOSING rightly may not restore reputation
The flood reunites them~Maggie rows to rescue Tom during a flood. Brother and sister briefly recover their bond before dying together, leaving reconciliation inseparable from the destruction of their possible future.~Maggie Tulliver,Tom Tulliver~storm~THE RIVER joins what judgement divided''',"Why does Maggie return from the journey with Stephen?","She refuses a future that would injure Lucy and Philip, despite her own strong attachment.",6688)
    A("Silas Marner","George Eliot",1861,"victorian","Novel","Lantern Yard and Raveloe","A lost hoard becomes the opening for a human bond.","Silas Marner=Weaver betrayed and isolated|Eppie=Child he raises|Godfrey Cass=Her concealed biological father|Dunstan Cass=Godfrey's reckless brother|Dolly Winthrop=Supportive neighbour", '''
Trust is broken~Silas is falsely accused of theft in his religious community. The betrayal destroys his trust in both people and faith, and he leaves for Raveloe with little expectation of belonging.~Silas Marner~letter~INJUSTICE creates withdrawal
Work and gold replace companionship~The weaver's skilled labour produces a growing hoard. Counting money becomes a narrow substitute for relationship, organising his life without answering the emotional need beneath it.~Silas Marner~coins~POSSESSION substitutes for connection
Another family's secret~Godfrey conceals a marriage to Molly while hoping to marry Nancy. Dunstan exploits his vulnerability, showing how respectable appearances can depend upon withholding another person's life.~Godfrey Cass,Dunstan Cass~mask~THE PUBLIC face has a cost
The gold disappears~Dunstan steals Silas's money and vanishes. Silas seeks help from neighbours, and the loss that first seems purely destructive begins to draw him into communal attention.~Silas Marner,Dunstan Cass,Dolly Winthrop~coins~LOSS makes an opening
The child enters~Molly dies outside, and her little daughter wanders into Silas's cottage. He keeps and names her Eppie, choosing responsibility rather than allowing the encounter to remain a passing interruption.~Silas Marner,Eppie~home~A LIFE replaces the hoard
Years of shared care~Raising Eppie with Dolly's help reconnects Silas with ordinary social life. The emotional change is built through repeated practical care, not through the instant disappearance of his earlier suffering.~Silas Marner,Eppie,Dolly Winthrop~garden~BELONGING grows through care
The claim is refused~Dunstan's remains and the gold are discovered; Godfrey acknowledges Eppie and offers wealth. She chooses Silas, making lived affection more significant than biological authority and economic advantage.~Silas Marner,Eppie,Godfrey Cass~heart~PARENTHOOD is also what people do''',"Why does Eppie refuse Godfrey's offer?","Her lived bond with Silas matters more than the wealth and status Godfrey can provide.",550)
    A("The Picture of Dorian Gray","Oscar Wilde",1890,"victorian","Gothic philosophical novel","London society and concealed rooms","A beautiful face outsources its conscience to a picture.","Dorian Gray=Young man whose portrait changes|Basil Hallward=Painter who loves his subject|Lord Henry=Provocative influence|Sibyl Vane=Actress Dorian idealises", '''
The portrait is admired~Basil paints the beautiful Dorian while Lord Henry speaks of youth and pleasure. Dorian begins to see ageing as a loss so severe that he wishes the portrait could bear it for him.~Dorian Gray,Basil Hallward,Lord Henry~mirror~BEAUTY becomes a bargain
The actress becomes an image~Dorian falls for Sibyl's theatrical performances. When real love changes her acting, he rejects her, exposing his attachment to aesthetic appearance rather than to the person before him.~Dorian Gray,Sibyl Vane~stage~LOVE confused with performance
The canvas records cruelty~After Sibyl's death, Dorian sees a change in the portrait. His own face remains young, allowing the consequences of conduct to be hidden in an object rather than displayed publicly.~Dorian Gray,Sibyl Vane~grave~THE IMAGE keeps the moral record
The room holds the secret~Dorian conceals the painting and pursues sensation while rumours gather. A corrupting book and Henry's influence matter, but they do not eliminate his responsibility for his choices.~Dorian Gray,Lord Henry~book~SECRECY protects the public mask
The artist sees the truth~Basil asks about Dorian's damaged reputation and is shown the portrait. Dorian murders the person who confronts him with the visible result of his life.~Dorian Gray,Basil Hallward~dagger~THE WITNESS becomes a target
Fear follows pleasure~Blackmail, memory and the threat from Sibyl's brother undermine Dorian's freedom. Preserved beauty has not preserved peace, and the concealed conscience continues to disturb him.~Dorian Gray~storm~ESCAPE from appearance is not escape from guilt
The portrait is attacked~Dorian stabs the canvas to destroy its accusation. He dies marked by age and corruption while the picture regains its beauty, returning the moral record to the body that tried to disown it.~Dorian Gray~mirror~THE RECORD returns to its owner''',"What does the portrait reveal that Dorian's face conceals?","The ageing and moral damage produced by his conduct.",174,"Magazine version 1890; expanded book 1891. Lord Henry's epigrams are character speech, not automatically the author's moral endorsement.",date="1890; expanded 1891")
    A("The Importance of Being Earnest","Oscar Wilde",1895,"victorian","Comedy of manners","London and an English country house","Invented identities expose society's very serious absurdities.","Jack Worthing=Invents a brother called Ernest|Algernon Moncrieff=Invents an invalid friend|Gwendolen Fairfax=Loves the name Ernest|Cecily Cardew=Imagines a romance with Ernest|Lady Bracknell=Guards social eligibility|Miss Prism=Holds the past's crucial clue", '''
An identity for escape~Jack uses the name Ernest in town while posing as a responsible guardian in the country. Algernon has his own fictitious excuse for avoiding duties, turning respectable conduct into a managed performance.~Jack Worthing,Algernon Moncrieff~mask~SERIOUS lives need an escape
A proposal meets a questionnaire~Jack proposes to Gwendolen, but Lady Bracknell investigates his eligibility. His discovery as an infant in a handbag threatens the marriage more than any evidence of his actual character.~Jack Worthing,Gwendolen Fairfax,Lady Bracknell~contract~BIRTH outranks behaviour
Algernon enters the fiction~Algernon visits Jack's country home pretending to be the invented brother Ernest. Cecily already has an imagined relationship with that figure, so his deception enters a romance written in advance.~Algernon Moncrieff,Cecily Cardew~book~A FICTION finds its admirer
Two engagements collide~Gwendolen and Cecily compare their claims to an Ernest. Polite conversation turns hostile before the exposure of both men's names redirects their anger toward the deceivers.~Gwendolen Fairfax,Cecily Cardew~heart~A NAME does the work of a person
The men try to repair the label~Jack and Algernon seek christenings to secure the desired name. The ridiculous solution makes society's declared seriousness about truth look subordinate to a preference for an attractive word.~Jack Worthing,Algernon Moncrieff~temple~CHANGE the label keep the desire
Miss Prism remembers the handbag~The governess's past mistake explains Jack's origins: she exchanged a baby and a manuscript. A domestic accident supplies the ancestry Lady Bracknell demands and connects Jack to Algernon's family.~Miss Prism,Jack Worthing,Lady Bracknell~letter~THE ACCIDENT restores pedigree
Earnest at last~Jack learns that his actual given name is Ernest. The ending rewards the supposedly invented identity while inviting laughter at a social world in which truth and respectability can depend on coincidence.~Jack Worthing,Gwendolen Fairfax,Algernon Moncrieff~stage~TRUTH arrives through absurdity''',"Why does the handbag matter?","It is the clue to Jack's parentage and the social eligibility controlling his marriage.",844)
    A("Strange Case of Dr Jekyll and Mr Hyde","Robert Louis Stevenson",1886,"victorian","Gothic novella","London streets and a divided household","A respectable man gives his hidden impulses a separate body.","Dr. Jekyll=Scientist attempting moral separation|Mr. Hyde=Embodied destructive impulses|Utterson=Lawyer investigating the mystery|Dr. Lanyon=Friend who witnesses a transformation|Poole=Jekyll's servant", '''
The troubling stranger~Accounts of Hyde's brutality introduce a figure associated with a respectable house. Utterson's curiosity is sharpened by Jekyll's will, which grants the disturbing stranger unusual power over his affairs.~Mr. Hyde,Utterson~gate~A RESPECTABLE door hides violence
Jekyll reassures the lawyer~Utterson suspects coercion, but Jekyll insists he can end the arrangement. The reassurance depends upon a confidence in control that the unfolding story will steadily undermine.~Dr. Jekyll,Utterson~contract~CONTROL is claimed too easily
The crime escalates~Hyde murders Sir Danvers Carew and disappears. Jekyll seems to return to sociable respectability, inviting the hope that the secret problem can simply be left behind.~Mr. Hyde,Dr. Jekyll~dagger~SUPPRESSED violence breaks outward
The friend is shattered~Lanyon experiences a revelation that destroys his confidence and health. His withheld account makes knowledge itself frightening, delaying the explanation while recording its human cost.~Dr. Lanyon,Utterson~letter~THE TRUTH harms its witness
The laboratory is forced~Poole fears that something has happened to his master. With Utterson he breaks into the laboratory, finding Hyde dead in circumstances that seem to leave Jekyll missing.~Poole,Utterson,Mr. Hyde~laboratory~THE ROOM contains the answer
The transformation explained~Lanyon's testimony reveals that Hyde can become Jekyll. Jekyll's own confession describes a potion intended to separate his moral identities, exposing the investigator's mistaken assumption that they were independent men.~Dr. Jekyll,Mr. Hyde,Dr. Lanyon~mirror~TWO NAMES one divided self
The experiment loses its limits~Transformations become involuntary and the necessary chemical cannot reliably be reproduced. Jekyll's attempt to outsource responsibility ends in the destruction of the self he meant to protect.~Dr. Jekyll,Mr. Hyde~chains~DIVISION does not remove responsibility''',"What is wrong with treating Hyde as simply another man?","He is a transformed expression of Jekyll, whose experiment tries to separate impulses without escaping responsibility.",43)
    A("Dracula","Bram Stoker",1897,"victorian","Gothic novel","Transylvania, Whitby and London","A scattered set of records becomes the weapon against a hidden predator.","Count Dracula=Vampire seeking new victims|Jonathan Harker=Solicitor trapped in the castle|Mina Harker=Organises knowledge and resists infection|Lucy Westenra=Early English victim|Van Helsing=Leads the investigation|Dr. Seward=Doctor recording events", '''
The solicitor enters the castle~Jonathan travels to Transylvania to assist Dracula's property purchase. Hospitality gradually reveals imprisonment, and strange encounters force him to recognise that his client is no ordinary nobleman.~Jonathan Harker,Count Dracula~castle~BUSINESS opens a dangerous door
The threat crosses the sea~Dracula travels toward England aboard a doomed ship. In Whitby, Lucy's sleepwalking and mysterious decline connect the foreign arrival to an intimate household danger.~Count Dracula,Lucy Westenra~ship~THE PREDATOR enters familiar life
Medicine meets the unexplained~Seward and Van Helsing try to save Lucy with transfusions and protective measures. Conventional knowledge alone cannot account for the attacks, and mistakes allow the threat to continue.~Lucy Westenra,Van Helsing,Dr. Seward~laboratory~KNOWLEDGE must change its assumptions
Lucy becomes another kind of victim~After death Lucy appears as a vampire attacking children. The group destroys her vampiric form, presenting release through a violence they believe necessary to restore her humanity.~Lucy Westenra,Van Helsing~grave~DEATH has not ended the harm
Mina assembles the evidence~Diaries, letters, reports and recordings become a shared account. Mina's intellectual labour turns isolated frightening experiences into a pattern that supports coordinated action.~Mina Harker,Jonathan Harker,Dr. Seward~book~COLLECTED records create power
The infection reaches Mina~Dracula attacks Mina and creates a bond that both endangers her and assists the pursuit. Excluding her from information proves dangerous, complicating the group's protective intentions.~Mina Harker,Count Dracula~chains~PROTECTION must include participation
The chase closes the circle~The pursuers follow Dracula back toward Transylvania and destroy him before sunset completes his escape. Mina's release and the later family record turn the struggle into a remembered collective achievement.~Mina Harker,Jonathan Harker,Van Helsing~road~COOPERATION defeats the hidden threat''',"Why are the diaries and records essential?","They connect separate experiences and let the group identify and pursue Dracula together.",345,"The novel's fears about gender, foreignness and sexuality require interpretation alongside its adventure plot.")
    A("A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man","James Joyce",1916,"modern","Modernist coming-of-age novel","Ireland and the imagined path toward exile","A young writer must learn to distinguish inherited voices from his own.","Stephen Dedalus=Developing artist|Simon Dedalus=His father|Dante=Family voice of religious loyalty|Cranly=Friend who tests Stephen's choices", '''
Language begins in childhood~Stephen's earliest world arrives through songs, stories and bodily sensations. The prose changes with his age, letting readers experience a developing consciousness rather than simply hear an adult summary of it.~Stephen Dedalus~book~THE STYLE grows with the child
The family world divides~Political and religious conflict erupts around him, including the Christmas dinner dispute over Parnell. Voices of authority reveal that belonging can demand incompatible loyalties.~Stephen Dedalus,Simon Dedalus,Dante~hall~AUTHORITY disagrees with itself
Humiliation and resistance~School discipline and family decline make Stephen vulnerable to shame. Complaining against an unjust punishment offers an early experience of using his voice rather than accepting every judgement silently.~Stephen Dedalus~chains~THE VOICE begins to answer back
Desire becomes religious terror~Sexual experience is followed by intense fear during sermons about sin and hell. Stephen embraces strict devotion, attempting to organise his uncertain life through an absolute moral system.~Stephen Dedalus~fire~GUILT seeks a complete system
The offered vocation is refused~The possibility of priesthood briefly promises status and security. Stephen recognises that such a role would not satisfy his developing relation to sensation, language and imaginative freedom.~Stephen Dedalus~gate~A PRESTIGIOUS role may be the wrong life
Beauty points toward art~An encounter with a girl near the sea becomes an imaginative revelation. Stephen begins developing ideas about artistic form, seeking a vocation that transforms experience rather than suppresses it.~Stephen Dedalus~sea~PERCEPTION becomes a calling
The artist chooses departure~Conversations with Cranly test the costs of rejecting conventional loyalties. Stephen prepares to leave Ireland, treating exile as a beginning whose success the novel does not guarantee.~Stephen Dedalus,Cranly~road~FREEDOM is also a risk''',"Why does the prose style change across the novel?","It follows the growth of Stephen's consciousness and language.",4217,"Serialised 1914–1915; book 1916. Stephen is a fictional artist, not a transparent substitute for every view of Joyce.")
    A("Dubliners","James Joyce",1914,"modern","Short-story collection","Dublin at the turn of the twentieth century","Small revelations expose lives unable to move.","The boy in Araby=Young admirer facing disillusion|Eveline=Woman hesitating before escape|Little Chandler=Dreams of another life|Gabriel Conroy=Central figure in The Dead|Gretta Conroy=His wife with a powerful memory", '''
A city in separate stories~The collection moves through childhood, adolescence, adult life and public institutions. Its characters belong to different stories; the linking thread is a city whose habits often limit action and self-understanding.~The boy in Araby,Eveline~city~ONE CITY many separate lives
The bazaar disappoints~In Araby, a boy imagines a purchase as an expression of romantic devotion. Arriving late at the commercial bazaar exposes the gap between his exalted fantasy and the ordinary world.~The boy in Araby~coins~DESIRE meets the unromantic real
The departure is refused~Eveline considers leaving with Frank but cannot step into the promised future. Duty, fear and memory turn the edge of escape into a scene of immobilisation.~Eveline~ship~THE DOOR opens but she cannot move
Another career is imagined~Little Chandler compares himself with a successful friend in A Little Cloud. His yearning for literary distinction collides with insecurity and domestic responsibility, making disappointment recoil upon his family.~Little Chandler~book~COMPARISON deepens frustration
Public life repeats the pattern~Later stories connect private weakness to politics, religion and institutions. No single villain explains the paralysis; social habits and individual evasions reinforce each other.~Eveline,Little Chandler~village~THE CITY shares its constraints
The party seems secure~The Dead places Gabriel within a warm gathering of music and family ritual. His speech and social confidence cannot prepare him for the intimate knowledge his wife will reveal.~Gabriel Conroy,Gretta Conroy~hall~CONFIDENCE rests on incomplete knowledge
The remembered lover changes the view~Gretta recalls Michael Furey, who loved her in youth and died. Gabriel's jealousy gives way to a widened reflection on love and mortality as snow links living and dead.~Gabriel Conroy,Gretta Conroy~grave~AN EPIPHANY enlarges the self''',"What is an epiphany in these stories?","A sudden recognition that exposes a character's life or self-understanding, often without guaranteeing decisive change.",2814,"Fifteen distinct stories. Frames select examples rather than inventing a single continuous plot.")
    A("Mrs Dalloway","Virginia Woolf",1925,"modern","Modernist novel","London during a June day after World War I","One day holds the hidden lives behind ordinary appearances.","Clarissa Dalloway=Preparing an evening party|Septimus Warren Smith=Veteran experiencing severe distress|Rezia=His isolated wife|Peter Walsh=Visitor from Clarissa's past|Sally Seton=Remembered friend|Sir William Bradshaw=Doctor enforcing conformity", '''
The flowers begin a day~Clarissa goes out to buy flowers for her party. London sights and sounds open memories, so a simple errand contains earlier desires, decisions and versions of the self.~Clarissa Dalloway~garden~THE PRESENT contains the past
Peter returns~Peter Walsh's visit unsettles Clarissa's account of her marriage and choices. Their recollections reveal affection, irritation and the continuing life of possibilities that were not taken.~Clarissa Dalloway,Peter Walsh~heart~AN OLD choice is not finished inwardly
Another life hears the city~Elsewhere Septimus struggles with wartime trauma and visions while Rezia seeks help. His distress runs beside Clarissa's day, showing an experience society cannot understand merely by observing behaviour.~Septimus Warren Smith,Rezia~city~THE SAME city different inner worlds
Authority calls it proportion~Doctors respond with reassurance or demands for control and separation. The language of health can become coercive when it refuses the particular suffering of the person seeking care.~Septimus Warren Smith,Sir William Bradshaw~chains~CARE can become domination
A fragile domestic moment~Septimus and Rezia briefly share warmth and practical work. The interruption of this connection by the prospect of compulsory treatment makes the threat of losing agency more immediate.~Septimus Warren Smith,Rezia~home~CONNECTION needs room to exist
The death enters the party~Septimus dies by suicide, and the Bradshaws bring news to Clarissa's gathering. Though she never meets him, the report interrupts her social performance and leads to solitary reflection.~Clarissa Dalloway,Sir William Bradshaw~grave~AN UNKNOWN life changes the room
Clarissa returns~She reflects on death, privacy and the pressures placed upon individual existence, then rejoins the party. The ending values connection without claiming that reflection can undo Septimus's loss.~Clarissa Dalloway,Peter Walsh,Sally Seton~hall~LIVING continues beside an irreparable absence''',"How are Clarissa and Septimus connected?","Through parallel concerns about inner life and social pressure, and finally through the news of his death at her party.",71865,"The suicide is not presented as a universal solution or as a simple result of one encounter. Stream of consciousness connects separate lives.")
    A("To the Lighthouse","Virginia Woolf",1927,"modern","Modernist novel","The Ramsays' holiday house in the Hebrides","A postponed journey becomes a way to measure loss and artistic vision.","Mrs. Ramsay=Creates moments of social connection|Mr. Ramsay=Anxious father seeking reassurance|James Ramsay=Child longing for the lighthouse|Lily Briscoe=Painter seeking her own vision|Cam Ramsay=James's sister", '''
The promised trip is denied~James hopes to visit the lighthouse, and his mother encourages the possibility. His father's weather prediction destroys the hope, making a small dispute carry the child's intense feelings about both parents.~James Ramsay,Mrs. Ramsay,Mr. Ramsay~sea~A SMALL plan holds a family conflict
Lily tries to paint~Lily works on a picture while resisting assumptions about women's artistic ability. Her struggle is to find the relation and form she sees, not merely to reproduce a familiar surface.~Lily Briscoe,Mrs. Ramsay~mirror~ART needs an independent vision
The dinner gathers lives~Mrs. Ramsay brings disparate guests into a temporary shared moment. The achievement is valuable precisely because she cannot make such connection permanent or eliminate everyone's loneliness.~Mrs. Ramsay,Mr. Ramsay,Lily Briscoe~hall~TOGETHERNESS is a fragile making
Time passes through the house~A largely empty house experiences weather, decay and war. Mrs. Ramsay and two children die, reported with startling brevity, while the material setting continues beyond individual lives.~Mrs. Ramsay~clock~TIME does not pause for the central figure
The family returns~Years later the surviving Ramsays revisit the house. Memory shapes the presence of every object, and the lighthouse journey resumes under circumstances the original child could not have imagined.~James Ramsay,Cam Ramsay,Mr. Ramsay~home~RETURN contains an absence
The journey reaches its mark~James and Cam sail with their father and gradually reconsider him. Arrival does not erase resentment or grief, but a changed relation becomes possible through the shared experience.~James Ramsay,Cam Ramsay,Mr. Ramsay~ship~SEEING differently is a kind of arrival
The painting finds its line~Lily completes her composition as the boat reaches the lighthouse. Artistic form and physical journey achieve parallel moments of completion without resolving all that time has taken.~Lily Briscoe~book~VISION makes a moment hold''',"Why do the voyage and the painting matter together?","Both pursue a form of completion shaped by memory, changed perspective and loss.", "https://www.gutenberg.net.au/ebooks01/0100101h.html","Three sections: The Window, Time Passes and The Lighthouse. This source is Project Gutenberg Australia.")
    A("Sons and Lovers","D. H. Lawrence",1913,"modern","Psychological novel","A Nottinghamshire mining community","Intimacy becomes a struggle between attachment and independence.","Paul Morel=Young man and developing artist|Gertrude Morel=Mother invested in her sons|Walter Morel=Mining father|Miriam Leivers=Paul's intellectually intimate attachment|Clara Dawes=Another partner|William Morel=Paul's elder brother", '''
The household divides~Gertrude's marriage to Walter becomes unhappy, shaped by class difference, drink and frustrated expectations. She turns increasingly toward her children, making the household's emotional structure unequal and intense.~Gertrude Morel,Walter Morel~home~A MARRIAGE shapes other attachments
William carries the hope~The elder son moves away, apparently offering his mother a future beyond disappointment. His death intensifies her grief and redirects much of her emotional investment toward Paul.~Gertrude Morel,William Morel,Paul Morel~grave~LOSS redirects dependence
Paul develops a different life~Work and artistic interests give Paul possibilities beyond the mining household. His closeness to his mother nevertheless remains central to how he interprets both achievement and affection.~Paul Morel,Gertrude Morel~book~GROWTH remains tied to home
Miriam offers spiritual intimacy~Paul and Miriam share reading, thought and feeling. He experiences their closeness as both sustaining and demanding, while his mother regards the attachment with suspicion.~Paul Morel,Miriam Leivers,Gertrude Morel~heart~A BOND can feel like a claim
Clara offers another connection~Paul turns toward Clara in a relationship with a different sensual and social character. It does not simply solve his difficulty: intimacy remains troubled by division and competing needs.~Paul Morel,Clara Dawes~mirror~ANOTHER partner is not an automatic cure
The mother approaches death~Gertrude's illness brings Paul's dependence and distress into sharp focus. Her death removes a central attachment while leaving him uncertain how to organise a future without it.~Paul Morel,Gertrude Morel~bed~THE CENTRE is lost
The city offers an unresolved direction~Paul moves toward the lights of the town rather than surrendering to the darkness associated with loss. The ending provides a direction, not a completed resolution of his emotional conflicts.~Paul Morel~city~A FUTURE must still be made''',"What complicates Paul's romantic attachments?","His powerful bond with his mother interacts with his own divided desires and fear of being claimed by others.",217,"Psychological interpretations vary; the novel is not a diagnostic case report.")
    A("The Love Song of J. Alfred Prufrock","T. S. Eliot",1915,"modern","Dramatic monologue","A modern city and imagined social rooms","A man rehearses life so anxiously that he cannot enter it.","Prufrock=Self-conscious speaker|The imagined companion=Ambiguous addressee|The women=Social figures he imagines judging him", '''
An invitation into paralysis~Prufrock proposes a journey through an evening city, but the opening's unsettling imagery makes motion feel strangely immobilised. Invitation and reluctance coexist from the start.~Prufrock,The imagined companion~city~THE INVITATION already hesitates
The rooms repeat~Social gatherings and cultured conversation recur in his imagination. He experiences the rooms less as places of connection than as stages where his appearance and competence will be examined.~Prufrock,The women~hall~SOCIETY becomes an imagined tribunal
Time supplies an excuse~Prufrock tells himself there is ample time for decisions and revisions. Repetition turns the promise of future action into a means of avoiding action in the present.~Prufrock~clock~MORE TIME can mean more delay
The body feels observed~Ageing, clothes and anticipated remarks about his looks preoccupy him. The supposed gaze of others becomes an inward mechanism preventing straightforward self-expression.~Prufrock,The women~mirror~THE JUDGE lives inside the speaker
The question cannot be spoken~He imagines an important declaration followed by misunderstanding or dismissal. Rather than naming the question clearly, he repeatedly rehearses the failure that might follow it.~Prufrock~letter~ANTICIPATED failure prevents the attempt
No prince's role~Prufrock rejects the grandeur of Hamlet and imagines himself in a secondary, cautious part. The self-comparison mixes comic self-knowledge with resignation about the scale of his own life.~Prufrock~stage~THE SELF chooses a smaller role
The sea of imagination breaks~Mermaids and an underwater world offer another dream of belonging, but Prufrock doubts they sing for him. Human voices interrupt the vision, leaving desire unable to secure a place in reality.~Prufrock~sea~THE DREAM cannot shelter him forever''',"How does time become a trap for Prufrock?","The promise of time for endless revisions gives him a reason to keep postponing action.",1459,"First published 1915; collected in Prufrock and Other Observations in 1917. The imagined addressee and central question remain ambiguous.")
    A("Four Quartets","T. S. Eliot",1943,"modern","Four-poem sequence","Remembered places and spiritual landscapes","Time is reconsidered through attention, surrender and return.","The meditative voice=Reflects rather than narrating a single plot|The remembered figures=Appear in visionary and historical encounters", '''
Four places establish the sequence~Burnt Norton, East Coker, The Dry Salvages and Little Gidding each provide a place-linked meditation. Their recurring images create variations rather than chapters in one continuous character adventure.~The meditative voice~road~FOUR places one recurring inquiry
Burnt Norton and possibility~An imagined garden evokes paths not taken and moments beyond ordinary sequence. The poem explores how attention to the present might relate to an order not exhausted by chronological time.~The meditative voice,The remembered figures~garden~THE PRESENT is not simple
East Coker and humility~Birth, decay and generations of living connect beginnings with endings. The meditation questions confidence in accumulated knowledge and turns toward the humility needed to learn from limitation.~The meditative voice~home~KNOWING needs humility
The Dry Salvages and uncertainty~River and sea imagery situate human lives within forces beyond prediction. The poem examines attempts to master the future while pointing toward forms of attention and prayer that accept uncertainty.~The meditative voice~sea~THE FUTURE cannot be possessed
Little Gidding and historical fire~A place of devotion and wartime experience connect purification with destruction. A visionary encounter draws past voices into the speaker's confrontation with history and spiritual responsibility.~The meditative voice,The remembered figures~fire~HISTORY enters the inward life
Pattern rather than a straight line~Images and phrases return with altered meanings across the sequence. Repetition asks the reader to revisit assumptions, making reading itself resemble the disciplined attention the poems explore.~The meditative voice~book~RETURN changes understanding
Arrival becomes another beginning~The closing movement connects exploration, recognition and spiritual renewal. Its hope belongs to a Christian vision of reconciliation, rather than an easy claim that every historical wound has been repaired.~The meditative voice~stars~THE END can deepen the beginning''',"Why is the title musical?","Recurring images and changing variations link four separate meditations into a larger pattern.", "https://tseliot.com/poetry/four-quartets","Individual poems published 1936–1942; first collected in the United States in 1943 and Britain in 1944. Thematic frames, not a literal voyage.",date="1943; individual poems 1936–1942")
    A("The Second Coming","W. B. Yeats",1920,"modern","Visionary lyric","A disordered world and an imagined desert","A promised revelation arrives in a terrifying new shape.","The speaker=Witnesses disorder and a vision|The falcon=Image of lost connection|The rough beast=Figure of an approaching historical order", '''
The widening motion~A falcon circles beyond the falconer's command. The image opens a world in which established relations between guidance and action have ceased to hold.~The speaker,The falcon~stars~THE LINK is broken
The centre cannot maintain order~Violence and disintegration spread. The speaker's account moves from a single image toward a general crisis, making collapse appear as a change in the structure of the age.~The speaker~storm~DISORDER reaches the centre
Conviction is unevenly distributed~The poem contrasts the hesitant with those driven by destructive intensity. Moral judgement is sharpened by a world in which confidence and goodness no longer seem reliably connected.~The speaker~crown~CERTAINTY does not guarantee wisdom
A revelation is expected~The speaker reaches for the language of the Christian Second Coming. An inherited framework initially promises a way to understand the crisis and anticipate what follows it.~The speaker~book~THE OLD story offers a prediction
The desert produces another image~A sphinx-like figure appears in the vision, with a human head and lion's body. Its indifferent gaze makes the expected revelation feel alien and menacing rather than consoling.~The speaker,The rough beast~stone~THE VISION overturns the expectation
An age is turning~The speaker relates the new image to a long historical cycle disturbed by the earlier Christian birth. The poem draws on Yeats's symbolic history rather than supplying a literal timetable of events.~The speaker,The rough beast~clock~HISTORY changes its pattern
The final question~The ending asks what beast moves toward Bethlehem to be born. The question preserves dread and uncertainty, refusing a reassuring conclusion about the world emerging from catastrophe.~The rough beast,The speaker~road~A BIRTH may bring terror''',"Why is the title unsettling by the end?","The expected Christian renewal is replaced by the vision of a threatening and unfamiliar birth.", "https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poems/43290/the-second-coming","Written 1919; first published 1920. Symbolic history and apocalypse, not a factual prophecy.",date="1920; written 1919")
    A("Sailing to Byzantium","W. B. Yeats",1927,"modern","Meditative lyric","A natural world and imagined Byzantium","An ageing voice imagines a form that can outlast the body.","The speaker=Ageing seeker of artistic permanence|The sages=Figures in a sacred mosaic|The golden bird=Imagined form of the transformed speaker", '''
A world of young life~The opening surveys lovers, birds and fish absorbed in generation and sensation. The speaker sees a vibrant natural world in which ageing consciousness has little recognised place.~The speaker~garden~VITALITY does not answer every need
The neglected monuments~Immediate life seems to distract people from enduring works of mind and art. The contrast sets up a search for permanence without pretending that natural abundance is itself worthless.~The speaker~stone~LIVING is not the same as lasting
The aged body is questioned~The speaker describes physical decline, then imagines the soul answering it through song. Artistic and spiritual activity become possible responses to a body that cannot regain youth.~The speaker~mirror~THE BODY declines the voice can grow
The imagined voyage~He turns toward Byzantium, a city associated with sacred art and cultural continuity. The movement is a poetic search for another order of value, not an ordinary travel report.~The speaker~ship~A PLACE names another aspiration
The sages are addressed~Figures within a gold mosaic are asked to teach the speaker's soul. Their holy fire promises transformation, but the request includes the painful release of desires still tied to bodily life.~The speaker,The sages~fire~TRANSFORMATION asks for surrender
The golden bird is chosen~The speaker imagines an artificial bird made by goldsmiths rather than another natural body. Craft offers a form outside the ordinary cycle of birth and decay.~The speaker,The golden bird~stars~ARTIFICE resists the natural cycle
A song across time~The imagined bird sings of past, present and future. The ending preserves a wish for artistic permanence while keeping the transformation in the conditional realm of poetic imagination.~The golden bird~book~ART holds time in a song''',"Why does the speaker imagine becoming a golden bird?","A crafted form seems capable of sustaining song beyond the ageing and death of a natural body.", "https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poems/43291/sailing-to-byzantium","First published in October Blast in 1927; collected in The Tower in 1928. The voyage and transformation are imaginative.",date="1927; collected 1928")
